# Menu & Header Architecture

Three goals, in priority order:

1. **Self-contained token layer** — menu partials read menu tokens, not global
   `theme.json` variables.
2. **Single breakpoint control point** — one variable decides desktop nav versus
   mobile drawer.
3. **Keyboard-safe styling** — focus states paired with hover; open states driven
   by ARIA and JS, never hover alone.

## Split of responsibility

`src/sass/header/` holds the header **shell** — announcement bar, search row,
utility cluster, scroll behaviour. `src/sass/menu/` holds navigation — desktop
nav row, mega panels, mobile drawer, link types.

Both are `@use`'d once from `src/sass/index.scss`. Mega menu rules do not go in
the global `_mixins.scss`; menu-specific mixins belong in `menu/_mixins.scss`.
Header shell files load tokens via `header/_tools.scss`, not `../tools`.

## Breakpoint control

Define the menu breakpoint once, as a **key into `$breakpoints`**, then wrap it:

```scss
// menu/_variables.scss
$menu-breakpoint-key: navigation;

// menu/_mixins.scss
@use "sass:map";
@use "../settings" as *;
@use "variables" as *;

@mixin breakpoint-menu-up {
	@media screen and (min-width: #{map.get($breakpoints, $menu-breakpoint-key)}) { @content; }
}

@mixin breakpoint-menu-down {
	@media screen and (max-width: #{map.get($breakpoints, $menu-breakpoint-key)}) { @content; }
}

// readable aliases — use when intent matters more than direction
@mixin menu-desktop-only { @include breakpoint-menu-up { @content; } }
@mixin menu-mobile-only  { @include breakpoint-menu-down { @content; } }
```

Every desktop/mobile split inside menu partials uses these mixins — never a raw
`@media`, and never the global `breakpoint()` mixin. Moving the switch point then
means editing `$menu-breakpoint-key` alone.

Desktop and mobile label visibility is a **CSS toggle** inside these mixins, not
`wp_is_mobile()`. Server-side device detection breaks under page caching.

## Token tier

`menu/_variables.scss` defines `$menu-*` tokens referencing global Tier 1 tokens.
Component partials consume `$menu-*` only:

```scss
// menu/_variables.scss
$menu-nav-link-hover-color: $color-primary;

// menu/_desktop.scss
.site-header__nav-link:hover { color: $menu-nav-link-hover-color; }  // ✅
.site-header__nav-link:hover { color: $color-primary; }              // ❌ skips the tier
```

## Selector scope

All menu Sass scopes under `.site-header`, with BEM element names:

```
.site-header__mega-menu
.site-header__mobile-drawer
.site-header__nav-item--has-mega
```

**Never** bare `.mega-menu`, `.mobile-menu`, or `#mobile-menu`. Those collide with
commercial themes and plugins that may still be installed during a rebuild. A
nav walker will emit WordPress's own `.menu-item` classes — style them as
`.site-header .menu-item`, never as global `.menu-item`.

Menu locations are registered from constants rather than string literals, so a
rename stays mechanical.

## Open states must be keyboard-reachable

Mega panels open on **click/tap**: JS sets `aria-expanded` and an `.is-open`
class. CSS must not depend on `:hover` to reveal content.

```scss
.site-header__nav-item--has-mega {
	&.is-open .site-header__mega-menu,
	&[aria-expanded='true'] .site-header__mega-menu {
		// panel visible
	}

	// keep the panel usable while focus is inside it
	&:focus-within .site-header__mega-menu {
		// match the open styles
	}
}
```

`:hover` may *supplement* desktop styling, but must never be the only selector
that reveals content or communicates state.

Pair hover with keyboard focus everywhere, preferring `:focus-visible`:

```scss
// ✅
.site-header__nav-link {
	&:hover,
	&:focus-visible { color: $menu-nav-link-hover-color; }
}

// ❌ hover only — unreachable by keyboard
.site-header__nav-link:hover { /* ... */ }

// ❌ outline removed with no replacement
outline: none;
```

## Mobile drawer

- Open state on the drawer element via `[aria-hidden='false']`, plus a document-
  level class for scroll lock
- Body scroll lock belongs inside `menu-mobile-only`, so desktop is unaffected
- Focus trap is JS; CSS provides visible focus on drawer links and on the close
  and back buttons

